# Agent Config — Git Tracking Rules
#
# STRATEGY: Two-layer versioning
#
#   Layer 1 — Git (this file)
#     Tracks static config files: soul.md, skills.md, policy.md, memory.md, tools.json
#     + framework templates and core cross-agent docs
#     Use git for: production snapshots, code reviews, rollbacks across the whole stack
#
#   Layer 2 — File history system (agent_loader.py + /api/v1/agents/history)
#     Tracks EVERY save with a comment, inside .history/<filename>/
#     Covers both manual edits (browser dashboard) and agent auto-writes
#     Use file history for: granular per-save versioning, quick restore, audit trail
#
# Result: git commits stay clean (no noise from agent auto-writes).
# Full per-save history lives in .history/ and is accessible at localhost:8000/agents

# ── Ignore: file history (fine-grained, per-save — NOT git's job) ──────────
**/.history/

# ── Ignore: dynamic agent auto-write files ─────────────────────────────────
# These are auto-written by the agent after tasks and accumulate frequently.
# Full history is maintained by the .history/ system above.
# If you want to snapshot a particularly important reflection/learning,
# copy it into memory.md (which IS tracked) before committing.
**/reflection.md
**/learning.md
**/feedback.md
**/evaluation.md

# ── Track: static config files (soul, skills, policy, memory, tools) ───────
# These are explicitly NOT ignored — git tracks them normally.
# soul.md, skills.md, policy.md, memory.md, tools.json — all tracked.
# Changes to these should be committed with a meaningful message.
